> Full and compact graphs retain the8/5/3 design

Each temporal convolution is followed by BN and ReLU. Global time averaging
feeds a linear2-class head. Width128/256/128 gives264,962 learned scalars;
compact16/32/16 gives4,450. Both have14 learned leaves and8 separate
normalization/BN-state leaves. There is no dropout or feature engineering.
The native suite executes full backward and checks all14 learned derivatives,
plus independent host finite differences for all40 tiny-model coordinates.
Pre-BN biases can have near-zero batch-statistic derivatives because a constant
bias shift cancels in BN;finite zero gradients there are not a broken graph.
```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <print>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  namespace m = n::models;
  n::set_num_threads(2);
  n::NoGradGuard guard;
  std::print("| graph | learned scalars | leaves | normalization/BN leaves | conv1 | conv2 | "
             "conv3 | head |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---|---|---|---|\n");
  for (int width : {128, 16}) {
    n::ParameterStore p(443), state(449);
    const auto out =
        m::SeriesCnn(p, state, n::ones({2, 150, 1}), m::SeriesConfig{width, 0, 1});
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",
               width == 128 ? "full" : "compact", p.count(), p.size(), state.size(),
               out.stages[0].shape(), out.stages[1].shape(), out.stages[2].shape(),
               out.logits.shape());
  }
}
```
